{
  "borough": "London Borough of Barking and Dagenham",
  "application_type": "All Other",
  "decision": "Refused",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed development would result in an inadequately sized dwelling, lacking a sufficient GIA and providing no built-in storage facilities creating a restricted and confined house. Additionally bedroom 2 would fall short of the required space standards, leading to cramped sleeping accommodation.&nbsp;Consequently, the development would offer a substandard quality of accommodation, contrary to:&nbsp;DCLG Technical Housing Standards (nationally described space standard) (DCLG, March 2015) (as amended)Policy D4, D6 and Table 3.1 of the London Plan (March 2021)Policy DMD1&nbsp;of the London Borough of Barking and Dagenham’s Local Plan (September 2024)Housing Design Standards LPG (June 2023)",
    "By reason of its bulk, mass, scale, siting, and orientation, the proposed dwelling would introduce a discordant form of backland development that disrupts the established layout and spatial separation between terrace rows. The proposal fails to respect the prevailing character and rhythm of the area, resulting in overdevelopment of the original plot, contrary to:Paragraphs 131 and 135 of the National Planning Policy Framework (NPPF) (MHCLG, December 2024)Policies D1 and D4 of the London Plan (March 2021)Policies SP2, DMD1, DMD5 of the London Borough of Barking and Dagenham’s Local Plan (September 2024)",
    "The proposed development, by reason of its bulk, mass, scale and siting, would result in a detrimental loss of outlook, overshadowing and an overbearing impact to neighbouring properties, notably nos. 80, 80a and 94 Keir Hardie Way. As such, this is considered contrary to:Part D of policy D6 of the London Plan (March 2021)Policy DMD1 of the London Borough of Barking and Dagenham’s&nbsp;Local Plan (September 2024)",
    "The proposed development fails to demonstrate&nbsp;that additional demand for parking from the development could be adequately accommodated in the surrounding area. The proposal is therefore likely to add unacceptably to parking stress and congestion in the vicinity of the application site.&nbsp; As such, it fails to comply with the following policies&nbsp;which seek to ensure proposals show how car parking will be designed and managed and to take into consideration on-street parking availability and existing and planned public transport provision: National Planning Policy Framework (December 2024) Policies T1, T6 and T6.1 of the London Plan (March 2021)Policies DMT1 and&nbsp;DMT2 of the Barking and Dagenham Local Plan (September 2024)"
  ],
